  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nBrief upper level ridging is currently in place through the day, \nkeeping conditions mild and dry. As the region transitions to a\nmore westerly flow, ensembles depict shortwave energy moving\nacross northern MT Thursday into Friday. This pattern will work to\nincrease the surface pressure gradient across south central MT\nfavorable for gap winds. Paired with 700 mb winds 35-50 kts, this\nsetup will likely be favorable for strong winds across the\nfoothills west of Billings (Livingston, Big Timber and Harlowton).\nThere is currently a 50% chance for gusts to at least 60 mph for \nBig Timber through Harlowton and about a 60% chance for \nLivingston/Nye during this time frame. As a result, a High Wind \nWatch has been issued for Thursday evening through Friday \nevening. Use caution if traveling during this time as winds may \nmake driving difficult, especially for high profile vehicles.\nAdditionally, appreciable mountain snow is also expected late \nThursday through Sunday with this system. There is currently up \nto an 80% chance for at least a foot of snow in the \nAbsaroka/Beartooth mountains with about an 80% chance along the \nassociated foothills for at least 2 inches. A 60-80% chance for at\nleast 6 inches of snow exists for the Crazy and Bighorn \nmountains. There is still uncertainty regarding precipitation \namounts, so stay up to date with the forecast as amounts are \nbound to change. The National Blend of Models (NBM) has a spread \nof around 0-0.5\" of precip across the lower elevations of south \ncentral MT at this time. Most of this is expected to be rain or a \nrain/snow mix. Into next week, mild ridging and northwest flow \nbuild in with ensembles hinting at unsettled conditions \ncontinuing.\n\nHigh temperatures are expected to be in the 40s to low 50s through\nFriday, cooling into the upper 30s and low 40s for the remainder\nof the period. TS",
